WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:07.299
Sausage Party was a surprise hit in 2016,
but the R-rated animated sex comedy has generated

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:08.720
plenty of controversy.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.430
Interestingly, not all of the uproar has centered
on the film's on screen madness — there's

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.040
also plenty of back stage drama as well.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.400
Here's a look at the untold truth of Sausage
Party.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.980
It started as a punchline

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:26.990
Back when Seth Rogen, Jonah Hill, and Michael
Cera were promoting their 2007 hit Superbad,

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.619
Rogen says journalists would ask them what
their next movie was going to be.

00:00:30.619 --> 00:00:35.840
As a joke, they'd say "Sausage Party," reasoning
that a title like that would fit with journalists'

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:38.800
expectations of the kind of movie they'd make
next.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.850
Nearly a decade later, when Rogen had the
idea of making an R-rated cartoon, he kept

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:47.830
coming back to the name of that fake movie,
and ultimately he and his writing partner

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:52.280
Evan Goldberg devised a concept and a script
around the name.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.600
Nobody wanted to make it

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.870
After Rogen and Goldberg developed a story
and artwork, they teamed up with co-directors

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:01.190
Conrad Vernon and Nitrogen head Greg Tiernan.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:04.760
And then, well, nothing happened for years.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:09.360
They pitched the movie to literally dozens
of studios, and even considered seeking private

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.580
investors when they invariably got shut down.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:17.320
Even with Rogen and Jonah Hill attached to
star, there seemed to be no studio willing

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:21.690
to gamble on a raunchy adult cartoon tackling
religious themes.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:26.950
Rogen's mission of making an R-rated Pixar
movie dragged on for eight years, until the

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:32.440
team eventually got the support of Annapurna
and Sony Pictures, who apparently just really

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:34.670
wanted to see horny food come to life.

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.310
And, really, is that so wrong?

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.229
"You're all alive, and looking at me!

00:01:38.229 --> 00:01:39.229
With your..

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:40.229
With your gloves!

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:45.020
And little shoes, and your arms and your legs!"

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.690
The MPAA said NO

00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:51.960
The version of Sausage Party that ended up
in theaters pushed plenty of buttons, but

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.940
believe it or not, the movie's original cut
took things even further.

00:01:55.940 --> 00:02:01.549
Rogen told Howard Stern this was by design,
as they "added six things" to the climactic

00:02:01.549 --> 00:02:08.509
food orgy as "sacrificial lambs" to appease
the MPAA and avoid an NC-17 rating.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.580
Much to their surprise, though, most of those
things actually made it into the final cut.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.410
The board instead focused on just a single
bit: the hairy genitalia belonging to a piece

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.410
of pita bread, which they "digitally shaved"
in order to make an R rating.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.420
A whole new world

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.390
If you picked up a distinct Disney vibe while
watching Sausage Party, it probably wasn't

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.840
just because of the animation.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.700
Oscar-winning composer Alan Menken, whose
credits include such classics as The Little

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.770
Mermaid, Beauty and the Beast, and Aladdin,
was hired to compose the score, as well as

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.580
the show-stopping opening number.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:43.140
"In here!

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.080
We keep our wieners in our packages.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.200
That's how it is."

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.520
"It sucks, but that's the way our buns keep
fresh."

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.270
It may seem surprising to longtime Disney
fans.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:59.569
But according to Menken, his decision to take
the job wasn't much of a shock to people who

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:01.070
know him in real life.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:05.340
He told The Hollywood Reporter, "I remember
telling my daughters that I was doing a project,

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.810
Sausage Party, and they looked at me and went
'Dad?

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.150
Sausage Party?'

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:14.760
In real life, I'm actually a very R-rated
person."

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.250
It almost had a different ending

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:20.620
Rogen screened an early cut of Sausage Party
at the South-by-Southwest film festival in

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:25.470
the spring of 2016, telling attendees that
they were "truly seeing something that no

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:30.209
one will ever get to see in the world" — and
it turned out he was right in more ways than

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:31.209
one.

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:35.260
That version's ending found the characters
exiting the animated world and entering real

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.960
life, like in the last act of The Lego Movie.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:42.209
But Rogen ultimately opted to go a different
direction…for now.

00:03:42.209 --> 00:03:47.080
As he later told Fandango, they may actually
end up using that idea for a potential sequel.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:52.349
"The idea of a live-action/animated movie,
like a Who Framed Roger Rabbit?-style hybrid

00:03:52.349 --> 00:03:57.150
is also very exciting, mostly because Who
Framed Roger Rabbit? is one of my favorite

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:58.980
movies of all time."

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.569
Drawn and quartered

00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:04.720
Conditions may have been bad for some unlucky
food in Sausage Party, but if allegations

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.610
prove to be true, they weren't the only ones
to suffer.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:11.770
According to allegations from former workers
at Nitrogen Studios, the company responsible

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:17.260
for the animations kept its costs low by forcing
employees to work overtime for free under

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:19.449
the threat of losing their jobs.

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:23.460
Dozens of animators left the studio over the
course of the production, claiming that if

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.900
they didn't stay late or work the weekends
for free, their work would be given to someone

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:28.900
who would.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:33.810
The subsequent exodus of talent forced Nitrogen
to hire recently graduated students to complete

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:38.280
the film, while some animators claim Nitrogen
even threatened to smear their reputations

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:39.280
as well.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.510
Further, allegations have also surfaced that
many of the animators who worked on the project

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:43.730
were uncredited.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:49.070
Nitrogen has denied all claims, but it may
be worth noting that of the 83 Sausage Party

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:54.960
animators listed on IMDb, only 47 were listed
in the credits.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.090
Surprising Dory

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.300
Sausage Party poked some gentle fun at industry
leader Pixar by having the character Druggie

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:05.860
drive a car with a "Dixar" bumper sticker,
and the license plate A113, which is a recurring

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.190
Pixar Easter egg referring to the animation
classroom at the California Institute of the

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.259
Arts where a lot of animators got their start.

00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:17.000
But the biggest prank Sausage Party played
on Pixar was entirely accidental.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.270
Shortly after the monster hit Finding Dory
opened to record-breaking crowds in June 2016,

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:27.770
a theater full of kids at the Brenden Concord
14 movie house in California were accidentally

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:32.139
shown the ultra-filthy red band trailer for
Sausage Party.

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:33.280
Whoops!

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:37.550
The theater's VP of operations, Walter Eichinger,
quickly issued an apology.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.380
"Playing that trailer was a one-time honest
mistake by a theater manager moving screens

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:47.310
around in effort to accommodate several large
last-minute groups wanting to see Dory.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:51.620
We regret it, apologize for it, and we are
not happy that it happened."

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:53.210
But I will do that

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:59.169
Theatrical classic rock singer Meat Loaf makes
a cameo as — what else? — a boxed meatloaf.

00:05:59.169 --> 00:06:03.440
"I would do anything for love.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.380
I'd never lie to you and that's a fact."

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.220
Literally nobody else could work for that
role, so Rogen had no choice but to lock down

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:15.229
Mr. Loaf.

00:06:15.229 --> 00:06:20.900
During a Q&amp;A after a screening of Sausage
Party at the 2016 San Diego Comic-Con, Rogen

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.080
said he personally met with Meat Loaf to pitch
him the part.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.960
Afterward, Rogen says, he was met by "Just
20 seconds of silence, and then he said, 'Yeah,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:30.580
I get it.'"

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:33.940
"But I just won't do that.

00:06:33.949 --> 00:06:35.720
I would do anything…"

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:36.720
Diddy?

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:37.830
No, he didn't

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:42.350
Speaking of cameos, Sean "Puffy" Combs was
almost in the movie.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:46.889
Rogen's team approached Combs for a cameo,
to voice a bottle of Courvoisier cognac . But

00:06:46.889 --> 00:06:49.960
when it came time to record his lines, Diddy
dropped out.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.740
The reason: he didn't want to provide a voiceover
for a cartoon.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:58.460
Rogen says Combs understood that the movie
was about food come to life, but he apparently

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.930
thought it was live-action.

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:03.590
And he thought he and the other performers
would be wearing food costumes.

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:05.650
Now that would have been amazing.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:06.650
Thanks for watching!

